GRAPH <https://github.com/hidden-graph/starlayer/blob/main/packages/starlayer/starlayer/graph/query/version_directive.py> {
    ns33:version_directive.py dcterms:identifier "starlayer.graph.query.version_directive" ;
        a ns2:Module ;
        rdfs:comment """SPARQL 1.2 Query prologue's optional ``VERSION "label"`` directive
    (SPARQL 1.2 Query sec 4.3), e.g. the spec's own example::

        VERSION "1.2"
        PREFIX : <http://example/>
        SELECT ...

    Not real SPARQL 1.1 syntax at all — rdflib's parser (and
    ``starlayer.sparql``'s own grammar, which extends it) has no notion of this
    directive and raises a ``ParseException`` on it unconditionally, so it must
    always be stripped before ordinary parsing, regardless of pipeline or
    backend.

    **``VERSION`` is not always the first token.** Per the real grammar
    (``tests/graph/vendor/spec_snapshots/sparql12-query.txt``):
    ``Prologue ::= ( BaseDecl | PrefixDecl | VersionDecl )*`` - the three
    prologue declaration kinds may appear in *any* order and ``VersionDecl`` may
    appear more than once. That's why this module scans the whole leading
    prologue token-by-token (recognizing ``BaseDecl``/``PrefixDecl`` well enough
    to skip over them unmodified, removing only ``VersionDecl`` tokens wherever
    they appear) rather than anchor-matching ``VERSION`` as the very first
    token of the document, which the grammar does not guarantee.

    Kept as its own small, self-contained module (not part of the retired
    ``sparql12_to_11.py`` text rewriter it used to live in): a prologue-only
    token scan has none of the nested/ambiguous-structure fragility (ordering-
    sensitive splicing, join-order bugs) that motivated replacing the rest of
    that module with a real grammar/algebra pipeline — there's no *query body*
    structure to get wrong here, only the three known prologue declaration
    shapes to recognize and either pass through (``BaseDecl``/``PrefixDecl``) or
    remove (``VersionDecl``).

    See ``starlayer.graph.model.conformance`` for what happens with the extracted
    label (a warning, never a hard error, if it doesn't match what the query
    actually uses), and ``starlayer.graph.backends.native.check_native_version_conformance``
    for the equivalent used by the native rdf-1.2 backend, which sends VERSION
    straight through to the endpoint unmodified instead of stripping it.""" ;
        rdfs:label "version_directive" ;
        ns2:declares ns41:strip_version_directive, ns41:contains_triple_term ;
        ns2:filePath "/Users/johnclements/Documents/GitHub/hidden-graph/starlayer/packages/starlayer/starlayer/graph/query/version_directive.py" ;
        ns2:importsModule ns4:__future__, ns4:re .

    ns41:contains_triple_term dcterms:identifier "starlayer.graph.query.version_directive.contains_triple_term" ;
        a ns2:Function ;
        rdfs:comment """Recursively check whether a parsed SPARQL 1.2 parse tree/algebra
    contains a ``TripleTermNode`` anywhere — used for VERSION-directive
    conformance checking (see ``starlayer.graph.model.conformance``). More
    reliable than a text-presence heuristic: this walks the real parsed
    structure, so it can't be fooled by e.g. ``<<(`` appearing inside a
    string literal or comment.""" ;
        rdfs:label "contains_triple_term" ;
        ns2:calls ns41:contains_triple_term ;
        ns2:filePath "/Users/johnclements/Documents/GitHub/hidden-graph/starlayer/packages/starlayer/starlayer/graph/query/version_directive.py" ;
        ns2:hasParameter ns41:contains_triple_term.param.node ;
        ns2:hasReturnValue ns41:contains_triple_term.returns ;
        ns2:lineEnd 122 ;
        ns2:lineStart 97 ;
        ns2:signature "def contains_triple_term(node) -> bool" .

    ns41:contains_triple_term.param.node a ns2:Parameter ;
        rdfs:comment "A parse tree/algebra node, list, tuple, or leaf value to search." ;
        rdfs:label "node" ;
        ns2:parameterKind "positional" ;
        ns2:position 0 .

    ns41:contains_triple_term.returns a ns2:ReturnValue ;
        rdfs:comment "True if a ``TripleTermNode`` appears anywhere in ``node``." ;
        ns2:annotationText "bool" ;
        ns2:hasType ns4:builtins.bool .

    ns41:strip_version_directive dcterms:identifier "starlayer.graph.query.version_directive.strip_version_directive" ;
        a ns2:Function ;
        rdfs:comment """Strip every ``VERSION "label"`` directive out of the leading
    prologue, leaving any ``BASE``/``PREFIX`` declarations - and their
    original relative order - untouched.

    If more than one ``VersionDecl`` is present (legal per the grammar's own
    ``( ... )*``, though the spec gives no precedence rule for it), the
    *last* one scanned is returned - an arbitrary but documented choice for
    a case real queries are not expected to actually use.""" ;
        rdfs:label "strip_version_directive" ;
        ns2:filePath "/Users/johnclements/Documents/GitHub/hidden-graph/starlayer/packages/starlayer/starlayer/graph/query/version_directive.py" ;
        ns2:hasParameter ns41:strip_version_directive.param.query ;
        ns2:hasReturnValue ns41:strip_version_directive.returns ;
        ns2:lineEnd 94 ;
        ns2:lineStart 56 ;
        ns2:signature "def strip_version_directive(query: str) -> tuple[str, str | None]" .

    ns41:strip_version_directive.param.query a ns2:Parameter ;
        rdfs:comment "The raw SPARQL 1.2 query/update text, possibly carrying one or more leading ``VERSION`` directives." ;
        rdfs:label "query" ;
        ns2:annotationText "str" ;
        ns2:hasType ns4:builtins.str ;
        ns2:parameterKind "positional" ;
        ns2:position 0 .

    ns41:strip_version_directive.returns a ns2:ReturnValue ;
        rdfs:comment "A ``(stripped_query, label)`` tuple: the query text with every ``VersionDecl`` removed, and the declared version label, or ``None`` if no ``VERSION`` directive was present." ;
        ns2:annotationText "tuple[str, str | None]" ;
        ns2:hasType ns4:builtins.str, ns4:builtins.tuple .

    ns4:__future__ a ns2:ExternalModule .

    ns4:builtins.bool dcterms:identifier "builtins.bool" ;
        a ns2:ExternalType ;
        rdfs:label "bool" ;
        rdfs:subClassOf ns2:Type .

    ns4:builtins.str dcterms:identifier "builtins.str" ;
        a ns2:ExternalType ;
        rdfs:label "str" ;
        rdfs:subClassOf ns2:Type .

    ns4:builtins.tuple dcterms:identifier "builtins.tuple" ;
        a ns2:ExternalType ;
        rdfs:label "tuple" ;
        rdfs:subClassOf ns2:Type .

    ns4:re a ns2:ExternalModule .
}

GRAPH <https://github.com/hidden-graph/starlayer/blob/main/packages/starlayer/starlayer/graph/graph/owl_dl.py> {
    ns25:owl_dl.py dcterms:identifier "starlayer.graph.graph.owl_dl" ;
        a ns2:Module ;
        rdfs:comment """starlayer.graph.graph.owl_dl

    OWL 2 Direct Semantics reasoning via owlready2 + Java HermiT - a genuinely
    different computational model (tableau-based DL reasoning, with real
    disjunctive case-splitting and sound-and-complete consistency checking)
    from every other entailment regime StarLayerGraph.infer() supports, all of which run
    owlrl's forward-chaining rule engine. See infer()'s own docstring for the
    entailment=ENTAILMENT['OWL-Direct'] contract; this module is the bridge that makes it work.

    Deliberately HermiT only, never Pellet (owlready2 bundles both behind
    sync_reasoner()/sync_reasoner_pellet()): HermiT is LGPL, Pellet is AGPL - a
    materially stronger copyleft obligation - and one conformant OWL 2 DL
    reasoner is sufficient to cover this regime. sync_reasoner_pellet() is
    never called anywhere in this module.

    engine="hermit" (this module) is one of two engines infer(entailment=ENTAILMENT['OWL-Direct'])
    can dispatch to - see starlayer.graph.graph.owl_dl_rustdl for the other
    (engine="rustdl": no JVM, but sound-not-provably-complete rather than
    sound-and-complete - a real, permanent gap against this module's own
    guarantee, not a bug in either). Both engines' Python packages are optional
    extras (`pip install starlayer.graph[hermit]` / `[rustdl]`), not core
    dependencies - `owlready2` used to be core, moved to opt-in alongside
    `rustdl` for a consistent story across both rather than one being special.
    A real Java runtime on PATH is the one prerequisite `pip` alone still can't
    satisfy for this engine specifically: owlready2 bundles Java HermiT and
    needs a real JVM at runtime to run it - see `_require_java()` below.""" ;
        rdfs:label "owl_dl" ;
        ns2:declares ns30:_require_java, ns30:InconsistentOntologyError, ns30:_run_hermit_subprocess, ns30:classify_owl_dl, ns30:_complete_type_closure, ns30:_require_owlready2 ;
        ns2:filePath "/Users/johnclements/Documents/GitHub/hidden-graph/starlayer/packages/starlayer/starlayer/graph/graph/owl_dl.py" ;
        ns2:importsModule ns4:__future__, ns4:shutil, ns4:pathlib, ns4:tempfile, ns4:rdflib .

    ns30:InconsistentOntologyError dcterms:identifier "starlayer.graph.graph.owl_dl.InconsistentOntologyError" ;
        a ns2:Class ;
        rdfs:comment """Raised when entailment=ENTAILMENT['OWL-Direct'] finds self's data logically
    inconsistent under OWL 2 DL semantics - e.g. an individual typed as two
    classes declared owl:disjointWith each other. Unlike entailment=ENTAILMENT['OWL-RDF-Based']
    (whose consistency checking, via owlrl, is partial and silently embeds
    a synthetic err:error triple into the result instead of raising - see
    infer()'s own docstring), HermiT's check is sound and complete, so an
    inconsistency here is surfaced as an unmissable exception rather than
    left for the caller to notice on their own.""" ;
        rdfs:label "InconsistentOntologyError" ;
        rdfs:subClassOf ns4:builtins.RuntimeError ;
        ns2:filePath "/Users/johnclements/Documents/GitHub/hidden-graph/starlayer/packages/starlayer/starlayer/graph/graph/owl_dl.py" ;
        ns2:lineEnd 50 ;
        ns2:lineStart 41 .

    ns30:_complete_type_closure dcterms:identifier "starlayer.graph.graph.owl_dl._complete_type_closure" ;
        a ns2:Function ;
        rdfs:comment """HermiT's CLI realization output only reports each individual's
    *most specific* known type(s), not every ancestor class up the
    hierarchy (confirmed live: for a plain `Manager rdfs:subClassOf
    Employee` + `alice a Manager`, HermiT reports `Type(alice, Manager)`
    but never `Type(alice, Employee)`, even though the subClassOf edge
    itself - `SubClassOf(Manager, Employee)` - is reported and present in
    `expanded`). Every other entailment regime infer() supports (all owlrl-backed)
    gives the full transitive rdf:type closure, so this step restores that
    same guarantee here - walks rdfs:subClassOf transitively from each
    individual's reported type(s) and adds every ancestor class as an
    additional rdf:type, mutating `expanded` in place. Purely additive:
    never removes or changes an existing triple, only adds ones already
    implied by what HermiT already reported.""" ;
        rdfs:label "_complete_type_closure" ;
        ns2:calls ns30:_complete_type_closure._ancestors ;
        ns2:declares ns30:_complete_type_closure._ancestors ;
        ns2:filePath "/Users/johnclements/Documents/GitHub/hidden-graph/starlayer/packages/starlayer/starlayer/graph/graph/owl_dl.py" ;
        ns2:hasParameter ns30:_complete_type_closure.param.expanded ;
        ns2:hasReturnValue ns30:_complete_type_closure.returns ;
        ns2:isPrivate true ;
        ns2:lineEnd 237 ;
        ns2:lineStart 202 ;
        ns2:signature "def _complete_type_closure(expanded: Graph) -> None" .

    ns30:_complete_type_closure._ancestors dcterms:identifier "starlayer.graph.graph.owl_dl._complete_type_closure._ancestors" ;
        a ns2:Function ;
        rdfs:label "_ancestors" ;
        ns2:filePath "/Users/johnclements/Documents/GitHub/hidden-graph/starlayer/packages/starlayer/starlayer/graph/graph/owl_dl.py" ;
        ns2:hasParameter ns30:_complete_type_closure._ancestors.param.cls ;
        ns2:isPrivate true ;
        ns2:lineEnd 230 ;
        ns2:lineStart 221 ;
        ns2:signature "def _ancestors(cls)" .

    ns30:_complete_type_closure._ancestors.param.cls a ns2:Parameter ;
